# 8. API Example

This becomes especially useful when you start working with APIs.

Imagine:

```text id="z7m4pc"
Flutter App
     ↓
API request
     ↓
Internet
     ↓
Server
     ↓
JSON data
     ↓
Flutter UI
```

`FutureBuilder` can manage the UI while waiting for the API.

For example:

```dart id="c9v5rk"
Future<List<String>> getProducts() async {
  // API call
  return ["Laptop", "Phone", "Tablet"];
}
```

Then:

```dart id="s2k7qa"
FutureBuilder<List<String>>(
  future: getProducts(),

  builder: (context, snapshot) {

    if (snapshot.connectionState == ConnectionState.waiting) {
      return CircularProgressIndicator();
    }

    if (snapshot.hasError) {
      return Text("Failed to load products");
    }

    final products = snapshot.data!;

    return ListView.builder(
      itemCount: products.length,

      itemBuilder: (context, index) {
        return ListTile(
          title: Text(products[index]),
        );
      },
    );
  },
)
```

This is a very common pattern.

---

# 9. Important warning

Avoid doing this directly inside `build()` for a real API request:

```dart
future: getProducts(),
```

without considering that `build()` can run multiple times, which may cause the Future to be recreated and the operation to run repeatedly.

For important API work, it is often better to create/store the Future in `initState()`.

Example:

```dart id="u8k2mz"
late Future<List<String>> productsFuture;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();

  productsFuture = getProducts();
}
```

Then:

```dart id="v5q9xc"
FutureBuilder<List<String>>(
  future: productsFuture,
  builder: (context, snapshot) {
    // UI
  },
)
```

---

# 10. FutureBuilder vs setState

Both can update UI, but they solve different problems.

### `setState`

You already have the data and want to tell Flutter:

```text
"My state changed."
```

Example:

```dart
setState(() {
  count++;
});
```

### `FutureBuilder`

You are waiting for an asynchronous operation:

```text
"Give me the UI for loading,
success, or error."
```

Example:

```dart
FutureBuilder(
  future: getProducts(),
  builder: ...
)
```

---

# 11. Memory trick

```text
Future
→ Data will come later

FutureBuilder
→ Build UI based on that Future

waiting
→ Show loading

done
→ Show data

hasError
→ Show error

snapshot.data
→ Actual returned data
```

### Main pattern to remember

```dart
FutureBuilder(
  future: someFuture(),

  builder: (context, snapshot) {

    if (snapshot.connectionState == ConnectionState.waiting) {
      return CircularProgressIndicator();
    }

    if (snapshot.hasError) {
      return Text("Error");
    }

    return Text("${snapshot.data}");
  },
)
```

This is one of the important Flutter concepts for **API and asynchronous data handling**.
